<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 3.1 - Invertibility</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-invertible">
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>n\times n</m> matrix is called <term>invertible</term> if
                there is a matrix <m>B</m> such that <m>AB=I_n</m> where
                <m>I_n</m> is the <m>n\times n</m> identity matrix.
                The matrix <m>B</m> is called the <term>inverse</term> and
                denoted <m>A^{-1}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark>
        <p>
            Notice that we only define invertibility for matrices that have the
            same number of rows and columns in which case we say the matrix is
            <term>square</term>.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the matrices
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        A = 
                        \ds
                    \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                    2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
                    1 \amp 1 \amp 1
                    \end{array}\right]
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        B = \ds
                      \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    1 \amp 2\amp -4 \\
                    -1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \\
                    0 \amp -1 \amp 2
                    \end{array}\right]
                    </md>
                </p>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Define these matrices in Sage.
                    What is the product <m>AB</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>AB = I_3</m> (the <m>3\times 3</m> identity matrix).
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,2,
                2,2,1,
                1,1,1])
B = matrix(3,3,[1,2,-4,
                -1,-1,3,
                0,-1,2])
A*B
                    </input>
                    <output>
[1 0 0]
[0 1 0]
[0 0 1]
                    </output>
                </sage>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>A</m> invertible?
                    If so, what is its inverse <m>A^{-1}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>A</m> is invertible, and its inverse is
                    <m>{A^{-1} = B}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the product <m>BA</m>?
                    Is <m>B</m> invertible?
                    If so, what is its inverse <m>B^{-1}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>BA = I_3</m> (the <m>3\times 3</m> identity matrix).
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,2,
                2,2,1,
                1,1,1])
B = matrix(3,3,[1,2,-4,
                -1,-1,3,
                0,-1,2])
B*A
                    </input>
                    <output>
[1 0 0]
[0 1 0]
[0 0 1]
                    </output>
                </sage>

                <p>
                    <m>B</m> is invertible and its inverse is <m>{B^{-1}=A}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the solution to the equation
                    <m>A\xvec =
    \threevec{4}{-1}{4}</m> using <m>A^{-1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    If we multiply both sides by <m>B</m>, which is the inverse
                    of <m>A</m>, then we get
                    <md>
                        BA\xvec = B\threevec{4}{-1}{4} \\
                    \xvec = \threevec{-14}{9}{9}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
B = matrix(3,3,[1,2,-4,
                -1,-1,3,
                0,-1,2])
x = vector([4,-1,4])
B*x
                    </input>
                    <output>
(-14,9,9)
                    </output>
                </sage>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Remember that the product of matrices usually depends on the
                    order in which you multiply; that is, if <m>C</m> and
                    <m>D</m> are matrices, it usually happens that
                    <m>CD \neq DC</m>.
                    In this example, does it appear that order matters when
                    talking about inverses?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    The order doesn't matter when talking about inverses,
                    because both multiplications equal the same size identity
                    matrix.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's begin with the matrix <m>
    A = \mattwo1213
    </m>.
                We would like to know if its invertible; in other words, we'd
                like to know if we can find a matrix <m>B</m> such that
                <m>AB=I</m>.
                Let's denote <m>B</m> by
                <m>B = \left[
      \begin{array}{cc} \bvec_1 \amp \bvec_2
        \\ \end{array}\right].
    </m>
                This means that we need to solve
                <md>
                    AB =A\left[ \begin{array}{cc} \bvec_1 \amp \bvec_2
    \end{array}\right] =  \left[
      \begin{array}{cc}
        A\bvec_1 \amp A\bvec_2 \\
      \end{array}
    \right]
    = I =  \left[
      \begin{array}{cc}
        \evec_1 \amp \evec_2 \\
      \end{array}
    \right] = \mattwo1001 ,
                </md>
                where <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m> are the columns of the
                identity matrix.
                We therefore have the two equations
                <md>
                    A\bvec_1 = \evec_1=\twovec10,
    A\bvec_2 = \evec_2=\twovec01
                </md>
                that we can solve for <m>\bvec_1</m> and <m>\bvec_2</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using Gaussian elimination, solve the equation
                    <m>A\bvec_1 = \twovec10</m> to find <m>\bvec_1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                  \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
                    1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                  \end{array}\right] \amp \xrightarrow{(R2-R1) \to R2} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
                  \end{array}\right]
                  </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                  \amp \xrightarrow{(R1-2R2) \to R1} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
                  \end{array}\right], 
                  </mrow>
                    </md>
                    which yields the solution <m>{\bvec_1 = \twovec{3}{-1}.}</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using Gaussian elimination, solve the equation
                    <m>A\bvec_2 = \twovec01</m> to find <m>\bvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                  \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
                    1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                  \end{array}\right] \amp \xrightarrow{(R2-R1) \to R2} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
                  \end{array}\right]
                  </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                  \amp \xrightarrow{(R1-2R2) \to R1} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
                  \end{array}\right], 
                  </mrow>
                    </md>
                    which yields the solution <m>{\bvec_2 = \twovec{-2}{1}.}</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on the previous parts, what is the matrix <m>B</m>?
                    Is it true <m>AB = I</m>?
                    (Can multiply with Sage.)
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>B = \left[\begin{array}{rr}3 \amp -2 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right].</m>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Yes, it is true that <m>{AB = I_2.}</m>
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
A = matrix(2,2,[1,2,
                1,3])
B = matrix(2,2,[3,-2,
                -1,1])
A*B
                    </input>
                    <output>
[1 0]
[0 1]
                    </output>
                </sage>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>A</m> invertible?
                    If so, what is <m>A^{-1}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    Yes, <m>A</m> is invertible since it row reduces to the
                    identity matrix.
                    This is because there is a pivot in every row and every
                    column.
                    We also have a <m>B</m> matrix above such that
                    <m>AB = I_2</m>.
                    In this case,
                    <m>A^{-1} = B = \left[\begin{array}{rr}3 \amp -2 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>C = \mattwo{-2}14{-2}</m>, what happens when you try
                    to apply this process to find <m>C^{-1}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                  \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    -2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
                    4 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
                  \end{array}\right] \amp \xrightarrow{(R2+2R1) \to R2} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    -2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
                    0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                  \end{array}\right],
                  </mrow>
                    </md>
                    which has no solution!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's go back to <m>A = \mattwo1213</m>.
                    Solve <m>A\xvec = \twovec14</m> using Gaussian Elimination.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                  \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
                    1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\
                  \end{array}\right] \amp \xrightarrow{(R2-R1) \to R2} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
                  \end{array}\right]
                  </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                  \amp \xrightarrow{(R1-2R2) \to R1} \amp \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 
                    1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \\
                    0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
                  \end{array}\right], 
                  </mrow>
                    </md>
                    which yields the solution <m>{\xvec = \twovec{-5}{3}.}</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the matrix <m>B</m> you found above and find the product
                    <m>B\twovec14</m>.
                    What does this have to do with the solution to the equation
                    <m>A\xvec = \twovec14</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>{B\twovec14 = 1\twovec{3}{-1} + 4\twovec{-2}{1} = \twovec{-5}{3}.}</m>
                    This is the same as the solution to the equation
                    <m>A\xvec = \twovec14</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the matrix <m>B</m> to solve the equation
                    <m>A\xvec = \twovec3{14}.</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    To do this, we can multiply both sides of the equation by
                    <m>B</m> giving <m>{BA\xvec = B\twovec3{14}.}</m> However,
                    <m>BA = I</m>, so this yields
                    <m>{\xvec = B\twovec3{14}.}</m>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Therefore, the solution to <m>A\xvec = \twovec3{14}</m> is
                    <md>
                        \xvec = B\twovec3{14} = 3\twovec{3}{-1} + 14\twovec{-2}{1} = \twovec{-19}{11}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.
                We've seen that, if <m>A</m> is invertible with inverse
                <m>B</m>, then <m>{A\xvec = \bvec}</m> has the solution
                <m>{\xvec = B\bvec}</m>.
                This means that the equation <m>{A\xvec = \bvec}</m> is consistent
                for every vector <m>\bvec</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does this imply about the span of the columns of
                    <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    The span of the columns of <m>A</m> is all of
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> since for every <m>\bvec</m> there is a
                    solution to <m>A\xvec = \bvec</m>, which means that for
                    every <m>\bvec</m> there is a linear combination of the
                    columns of <m>A</m> that gives <m>\bvec</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does this imply about the pivot positions of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    The system is always consistent, so there is a pivot in
                    every row!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Since <m>A</m> is a square matrix - that is, <m>A</m> has
                    the same number of rows and columns - what is the reduced
                    row echelon form of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    We already know that there is a pivot in every row from
                    above, so because the matrix is square, there must also be a
                    pivot in every column.
                    Therefore, the reduced row echelon form of <m>A</m> is the
                    identity matrix of size <m>{n\times n}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the linear independence/dependence of
                    the columns of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="15-solutions">
                <p>
                    Since there is a pivot in every column, the columns of
                    <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Take
                <md>
                    A=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 3 \\ \end{bmatrix},~~~   C = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
    -2 \amp 1 \\
    4 \amp -2 \\
  \end{array}\right],~~~
                </md>
                Find the reduced row echelon form of these matrices.
                What does this tell you about the invertibility of these two
                matrices?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="15-solutions">
            <p>
                <m>A</m> row reduces to the <m>2\times 2</m> identity matrix and
                therefore, <m>A</m> is invertible.
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>C</m> row reduces to the matrix
                <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -\frac{1}{2} \\ 0 \amp 0 \\ \end{bmatrix}</m>.
                Thus, <m>C</m> is not invertible.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Given a matrix, how can one use row reduction to tell if the
                    matrix is invertible?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How can we use the inverse of a matrix <m>A</m> to solve
                    <m>Ax=b</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <!-- <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the inverse of a matrix using row reduction?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the inverse of a matrix using Sage?
                </p>
            </li> -->
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>